Subject: [PATCH] GS: host-to-local uploads left out while a native renderer draws alone

While ps2_host_display::gsNativeAlone() (nothing draws from GS memory), a host-to-local
upload in a colour or indexed format (CT32/24/16/16S, T8, T4) is not queued for the GS
thread: its data is kept in a shadow (each destination's last data, in order of the last
writes). It reaches GS memory when anything needs it: the emulated drawing back on (a lost
device), a local-to-host or local-to-local transfer, a VRAM snapshot (all of it, in order);
a reader of GS memory outside the GS (gsupload::ensureWhole: the native renderer's light
maps) has the destinations that reach into its region written first and their turn on the GS
thread waited for. Top-byte uploads (T8H/T4HL/T4HH: the light maps) and depth formats are
made as before (and recorded, so the replays keep their place). PS2X_GS_SKIP_UPLOADS=0:
every upload made, as before; =check: every upload made, and the blocks a skip would have
left stale marked - gsupload::staleAt / reportRead for readers. PS2X_GS_NATIVE_LOG=1 adds a
10 s count line. (Transformers roadmap N4: the uploads.)

diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/gs_threaded_backend.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/gs_threaded_backend.h
index 6e56280..22cf985 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/gs_threaded_backend.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/gs_threaded_backend.h
@@ -16,6 +16,35 @@
 // VRAM writes) are queued and return at once; calls that observe the result (VRAM reads,
 // snapshots, local-to-host transfers, presentation) first wait until the queue has been
 // processed up to that point. PS2X_GS_SYNC=1 keeps the backend on the calling thread.
+// (Roadmap N4, the uploads) While the native renderer draws alone, nothing draws from GS memory: the game's
+// host-to-local uploads of its textures - streamed into GS memory every frame, 30,000-100,000 transfers a second
+// in a level - are not made (PS2X_GS_SKIP_UPLOADS; the GS thread's swizzling and the ring's copy saved) except
+// those a reader needs: the top-byte formats (T8H/T4HL/T4HH - the light maps, which the native renderer reads
+// from GS memory) and the depth formats. What a skipped upload would have written is kept in a shadow (each
+// destination's last data) and written to GS memory in order the moment anything needs it whole: the emulated
+// drawing back on (a lost device), a local-to-host or local-to-local transfer, a state snapshot.
+// PS2X_GS_SKIP_UPLOADS=0: every upload made, as before; =check: every upload made, and GS memory's blocks that a
+// skip would have left stale are marked - readers ask staleAt() and report what they would have read wrong.
+namespace gsupload
+{
+    enum class Mode : uint8_t
+    {
+        Off,
+        On,
+        Check,
+    };
+    Mode mode();
+    // (check mode) Whether the byte at `address` in GS memory would be stale had the uploads been skipped:
+    // `topByte` = the reader reads the top byte of 32-bit words only (a T8H/T4HL/T4HH view: the light maps).
+    bool staleAt(uint32_t address, bool topByte);
+    // (check mode) A reader's verdict, counted and the first ones logged ("[gs:skip] check ...").
+    void reportRead(const char *what, uint32_t tbp, uint32_t psm, uint32_t staleBytes, uint32_t bytes);
+    // (on) A reader of GS memory (the native renderer's light maps, any thread): the bytes [first, last) made as the
+    // uploads would have left them - the skipped uploads that reach into them written in order, and their turn on
+    // the GS thread waited for - before it reads. Nothing to do (no wait) when none of them was left out.
+    void ensureWhole(uint64_t first, uint64_t last);
+}
+
